his scarcity meant that knowledge was concentrated in the hands of a small elite\u2014primarily the Church and the aristocracy\u2014and that errors could accumulate as manuscripts were repeatedly copied by hand.\n\nJohannes Gutenberg, a goldsmith from Mainz in the Holy Roman Empire, synthesized several existing technologies into a cohesive system. He developed a method for casting movable type using a hand mold that allowed for the rapid production of individual letters. He adapted oil-based in suitable for metal type, and he modified the agricultural screw press used for wine-making to apply even pressure to the paper. This integrated system dramatically reduced the cost and time required to produce books.\n\nThe immediate results were striking. Gutenberg's most famous product, the Gutenberg Bible, printed around 1455, demonstrated the quality achievable through the new method. Yet the true significance lay not in any single book but"}